# THE GUARD REVISION THIS REPOSITORY AUDITED, BY CONTENT.
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml pins the shared release guards by commit
# SHA. A SHA is content-addressed and therefore immutable, so the pin
# cannot be edited under us. What a SHA does NOT do is tell a reader what
# is in it, and nothing states which BYTES were looked at before pinning
# them. That is what this file is for.
#
# Every file the guards execute is recorded below by SHA-256, taken at
# the pinned revision. tests/test_guard_pins.sh re-fetches each one and
# refuses any difference, so:
#
#   * bumping the pin without re-auditing goes RED, because the digests
#     below describe a revision the workflow no longer names;
#   * a pin that resolves to bytes other than the audited ones goes RED,
#     which is the falsifiable form of "we ran what we pinned";
#   * the check runs offline of any claim the guards make about
#     themselves, before a tag is ever pushed.
#
# WHAT REMAINS TRUSTED, stated plainly rather than papered over: that
# GitHub runs the workflow file living at the pinned SHA. A workflow
# cannot establish its own identity from inside itself, because a
# substituted file could simply print the expected answer. That is the
# platform's guarantee and the reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA.
# Everything downstream of it is checked here by content.
#
# THE DIGESTS BELOW WERE WRITTEN BY `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh`, which
# fetched each file at 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and hashed it. Do not
# hand-edit them, and do not copy this file from another repository: a
# copied record passes every check while having audited nothing, because
# the digests are genuinely the pinned revision's bytes. What a copy
# cannot carry is the reading.
#
# TO RE-AUDIT AFTER A PIN BUMP: read the diff, then re-run
# `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh <this repository>` and rewrite the note below.
#
#   https://github.com/nelsonduarte/capa-language/compare/<old>...2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT NOTE.
#
# FIRST PIN FOR THIS REPOSITORY, so there is no predecessor revision and
# no diff to read. All five files below were fetched at
# 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and READ IN FULL (1171 lines:
# 608 + 80 + 112 + 223 + 148), not accepted on their digests. What
# follows is what that reading found, including the parts that are not
# reassuring.
#
# tools/capa_floor.sh chooses which RELEASED compiler the clean room
# downloads, by parsing the `capa` key of [package]. Two things it says
# at this pin are no longer true of the compiler:
#
#   * its header (lines 19-24) states that nothing in the compiler
#     ENFORCES the manifest floor and that this guard is "the only thing
#     that tests the claim". capa 1.19.0 changed that.
#   * its accepted grammar is `>=?[0-9][0-9.]*[0-9]`, which admits
#     two-component and four-component versions the compiler-side parser
#     now refuses.
#
# NEITHER REACHES THIS REPOSITORY. capa.toml declares `capa = ">=1.18.1"`,
# a plain three-component floor that both grammars read identically as
# 1.18.1, and it is the only `capa` key inside [package]. Moving the pin
# to pick up whatever has changed upstream is a fleet-wide decision and
# is deliberately not made here.
#
# tools/clean_room_build.sh runs the consumer flow this repository
# supplies, inside an empty directory, from the tarball's own bytes, with
# CAPA_PATH unset. It fails closed on zero --run commands, a non-empty
# room, a relative room, and a tarball that does not extract to exactly
# one top-level entry. Its header names the defect that is most relevant
# to THIS package: capa_authgate shipped a tarball whose .gitattributes
# export-ignored tools/ while its README told downloaders to run
# `python tools/nest_vendor.py`, so the documented command was
# unrunnable for everyone who was not us. capa_hash carries the same
# `tools/ export-ignore` line, and that was checked against this exact
# failure mode rather than assumed benign: tools/ holds only
# sha256_ref.py, a Python oracle the toolchain never loads, no consumer
# command in either workflow invokes anything under tools/, and the
# README states the export-ignore where it introduces the file. The line
# is honest here; the guard would catch it if that ever stopped being so,
# because the clean room runs the flow against the exported tarball.
#
# tools/check_tag_version.sh reads `version` from [package] only, refuses
# anything other than exactly one match, and refuses the release unless
# the tag is literally v<version>. capa.toml has one such line
# (0.2.0), so the first tag this gates must be v0.2.0.
#
# tools/verify_guard_digests.sh checks the fetched guard tree against the
# digests below. Its header is explicit that a workflow cannot establish
# its own identity from inside itself and that this buys only the
# run-time-fetched scripts; it rejects `..` in a recorded path and treats
# an empty digests file as an error rather than a pass.
#
# .github/workflows/release-guards.yml fails closed on an empty
# `job.workflow_sha` before any checkout, and on empty
# `consumer-commands`. It downloads the released compiler named by the
# floor and verifies that binary's own SLSA attestation before using it,
# and it hands the guards `contents: read` and no id-token.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

